Z||000000||BC-GT||12-30-2001||05:38 PM||||Was looking at ignition systems and would like someone to shed some light on the difference between the two types(MSD=capacitive) and (Crane=inductive)<BR>Pros and cons anyone(besides who had what and for how long it lasted type of post please, and thanks)||||reg||1
Z||000001||88CoupeV6||12-30-2001||06:42 PM||||I have the Crane HI-6 <A HREF="http://www.cranecams.com/ignition/hi6.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.cranecams.com/ignition/hi6.htm</A> <P>It is a CD ignition system. I think it has more features than MSD and You control the Rev limiter with knobs built in so you don't have to buy little modules. It was $150 I think.<P><BR>It is unreal how better the car feels. I can really bring the RPM's up quick and it is like a new engine. I love it.<P>------------------<BR>Josh<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88coupeV6.jpg">       <BR><A HREF="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/AlbumList?u=1249362" TARGET=_blank>My pics</A>    <A HREF="http://www.fieropride.com/nefa.htm" TARGET=_blank>Join New England Fiero Association</A>||||reg||7
Z||000002||Turbo88||12-30-2001||06:48 PM||||Well, a capacitive circuit uses capacitors to store and hold a charge until they are discharged. Think of it like taking a one gallon bucket of water and filling it slowly with a water hose. Then think of emptying it so fast, it is empty in a matter of nanoseconds. One gallon of water sprayed slowly out of a hose doesn't hit as hard as one gallon hitting you in an instant. Basicly you will have about the same voltage output as without but at many many times the amperage. That makes for a short, fat, hot, spark. <P>In inductive system uses resonate rise to increase the voltage to the moon. A Tesla Coil uses this principle. An inductive system will have a huge amount of voltage available which will create a long, skinny, cool, spark. Where this comes into play is that, for the same plug gap, the inductive system with it's higher voltages will overcome more air resistance. That means that it will have less trouble jumping the gap in a tough enviroment like a juiced, or blown engine will provide. The capacitive unit will create a much hotter spark but will have more trouble jumping the gap. <P>That is the difference at the fundimental level. As far as which manufacture is better, just understand that, at 40kv, the capacitive will have the same gap jumping ability but will provide a hotter spark. I don't know what a given manufacture is advertising. I dont know. I hope this helps!||||reg||1
Z||000003||BC-GT||12-30-2001||08:03 PM||||From what I understand then, both would be somewhat overkill on a bored out 2.8<BR>Will prolly go for a Crane HI-6S(inductive) just because it's 40 bucks cheaper. Does $175 CAN. sound reasonable?<BR>I don't forsee any problems running it with a MSD blaster coil as well, do I? <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/confused.gif">||||reg||1
